{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017ZaiDqWaZTRqt2v8H88mHf","type":"tool_result","content":"100\t  - `docs/03_technical/TESTING_PATTERNS.md`\n101\t\n102\t---\n103\t\n104\t### examples/ — Usage Examples\n105\t- `run_backtest.py` — Example script for executing backtests\n106\t\n107\t---\n108\t\n109\t### alembic/ — Database Migrations\n110\t- `versions/` — Schema migration scripts\n111\t- `env.py` — Alembic environment configuration\n112\t\n113\t---\n114\t\n115\t### .beads/ — Operational Memory (Beads)\n116\t\n117\t- `beads.db` — Local Beads database\n118\t- `config.yaml` — Beads configuration\n119\t- Used as the **task DAG and long-term memory** for work in progress\n120\t\n121\t---\n122\t\n123\t### Root Configuration & Tooling\n124\t\n125\t- `AGENTS.md` — Agent operating rules (this file)\n126\t- `CLAUDE.md` — Claude-specific agent rules (mirrors AGENTS.md)\n127\t- `docker-compose.yml`, `Dockerfile` — Local infrastructure\n128\t- `requirements*.txt`, `pyproject.toml` — Python dependencies\n129\t- `.env`, `.env.example` — Environment configuration\n130\t- `.vscode/`, `.github/` — IDE and CI configuration\n131\t\n132\t---\n133\t\n134\t## ULTRAIMPORTANT\n135\t\n136\tThink carefully and only action the specific task I have given you with the most concise and elegant solution that changes as little code as possible.\n137\tAvoid over-engineering. Only make changes that are directly requested or clearly necessary. Keep solutions simple and focused.\n138\t\n139\tDon't add features, refactor code, or make \"improvements\" beyond what was asked. A bug fix doesn't need surrounding code cleaned up. A simple feature doesn't need extra configurability.\n140\t\n141\tDon't add error handling, fallbacks, or validation for scenarios that can't happen. Trust internal code and framework guarantees. Only validate at system boundaries (user input, external APIs). Don't use backwards-compatibility shims when you can just change the code.\n142\t\n143\tDon't create helpers, utilities, or abstractions for one-time operations. Don't design for hypothetical future requirements. The right amount of complexity is the minimum needed for the current task. Reuse existing abstractions where possible and follow the DRY principle.\n144\t\n145\tDo not write tests with excessive mockups that do not test any solution code at all.\n146\t\n147\t---\n148\t\n149\t## Working System: Roles, Artifacts, and Handoffs (MVP)\n150\t\n151\tThis repo uses a **multi-agent workflow** based on **explicit artifacts** and **clear handoffs**. Agents should not rely on chat history as the source of truth; they should read and write the artifacts below.\n152\t\n153\tThere is **a single documentation tree** under `docs/`.  \n154\tNo parallel documentation systems are used.\n155\t\n156\t---\n157\t\n158\t## Documentation as a Single System (`docs/`)\n159\t\n160\tThe documentation lives in `docs/` folder.\n161\t\n162\tIt serves two complementary purposes:\n163\t1. **Durable documentation** (architecture, requirements, guides)\n164\t2. **Operational, per-change artifacts** produced by agents and tracked via Beads\n165\t\n166\tWhen generating documentation always divide between analysis or functional documentation and technical specification. Mainly, do not mix both in the same file, unless when a technical detail helps in the functional explanation.\n167\t\n168\tFallback to update existing documents. Try to not generate new documents all the time. We should have a main document for each one of the types described next and detail documents for specific requirements or technical decisions/implementations. The main type of documents should be:\n169\t- Requirements: Functional and UI specifications.\n170\t- Planning or task management: details of which are the plans and current status\n171\t- Technical specification: Architecture, Design, Decisions, Implementation, technical configurations.\n172\t\n173\tBoth live in the same tree and follow the same rules.\n174\t\n175\tStandards:\n176\t1. Prefer updating existing docs over creating new ones\n177\t2. Link between related documents; **do not repeat** the same content in multiple places\n178\t3. Code comments only when logic isn’t self-evident\n179\t\n180\t---\n181\t\n182\t### Documentation Structure\n183\t\n184\t```\n185\tdocs/\n186\t├─ 01_requirements/\n187\t│ ├─ README.md\n188\t│ ├─ trading_system_requirements.md\n189\t│ ├─ QuantAgent-a3f2dd-RQ-<short-slug>.md\n190\t│ └─ QuantAgent-b91c02-RQ-<short-slug>.md\n191\t│\n192\t├─ 02_planning/\n193\t│ ├─ README.md\n194\t│ ├─ phase1_roadmap.md\n195\t│ └─ QuantAgent-a3f2dd-PL-<short-slug>.md\n196\t│\n197\t├─ 03_design/\n198\t│ ├─ README.md\n199\t│ ├─ architecture.md"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2b9b65c5-e77e-400b-90aa-d1fae3699661","uuid":"11d667e5-4845-41a7-980d-24a542daf071","timestamp":"2026-05-09T02:39:19.475Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/azureuser/repos/projects/QuantAgent/AGENTS.md","content":"  - `docs/03_technical/TESTING_PATTERNS.md`\n\n---\n\n### examples/ — Usage Examples\n- `run_backtest.py` — Example script for executing backtests\n\n---\n\n### alembic/ — Database Migrations\n- `versions/` — Schema migration scripts\n- `env.py` — Alembic environment configuration\n\n---\n\n### .beads/ — Operational Memory (Beads)\n\n- `beads.db` — Local Beads database\n- `config.yaml` — Beads configuration\n- Used as the **task DAG and long-term memory** for work in progress\n\n---\n\n### Root Configuration & Tooling\n\n- `AGENTS.md` — Agent operating rules (this file)\n- `CLAUDE.md` — Claude-specific agent rules (mirrors AGENTS.md)\n- `docker-compose.yml`, `Dockerfile` — Local infrastructure\n- `requirements*.txt`, `pyproject.toml` — Python dependencies\n- `.env`, `.env.example` — Environment configuration\n- `.vscode/`, `.github/` — IDE and CI configuration\n\n---\n\n## ULTRAIMPORTANT\n\nThink carefully and only action the specific task I have given you with the most concise and elega