{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01M9uNfyLeHBkdUegyLuGf9x","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a complete picture. Let me synthesize the analysis:\n\n## Issue Analysis\n\n### Problem 1: Missing heartbeat methods in TradingScheduler\n\nThe tests in `test_vje_scheduler_heartbeat_backend.py` expect:\n- `TradingScheduler._upsert_heartbeat_start(timestamp)` → inserts/updates a `SchedulerHeartbeat` row per environment, returns the row\n- `TradingScheduler._upsert_heartbeat_complete(heartbeat, stats)` → updates the row with completion info (status='completed', stats, last_trade_id, completed_at)\n\n\n- `analyze_and_trade()` → calls these at start/end of cycle, continues gracefully if start fails\n\nCurrently, `TradingScheduler` has neither of these methods and `analyze_and_trade()` doesn't call them. The heartbeat feature was designed with a `SchedulerHeartbeat` model and tests, but never actually implemented in the scheduler.\n\n### Problem 2: DummySession.query() returns incomplete mock chain\n\nThe mock query object in `tests/trading/test_scheduler.py` doesn't properly chain the filter and first methods together, breaking the test setup.\n\nThe issue is that `PositionMonitor.get_active_position()` chains two filters and then calls `order_by(...).first()`, but the mock only sets up the first filter-to-first path. When the code chains a second filter and then calls order_by, it gets a fresh Mock instead of the configured return value, so the test doesn't properly simulate the database behavior.\n\nThe root cause is that `DummySession.query()` wasn't updated when `order_by()` was added to the call chain in `get_active_position`. I need to fix this by configuring the mock to handle the full chain including the `order_by` step.\n\nAdding heartbeat methods to TradingScheduler... Writing database update logic...\n\nActually, looking at the test more carefully, I see the upsert behavior needs to be different than what I wrote—it should maintain only one row per environment, not create a new one on each run. The first call creates the heartbeat record, and subsequent calls update that same row with new timestamps and status, rather than inserting duplicates.\n\nNow I'm looking at how the completion method works. It takes the heartbeat record that was created and updates it with final status, completion time, and stats from the run. The test shows it should mark the status as completed, set a completion timestamp, capture the trade ID that was processed, and store the run statistics like processed count and error count.\n\nThe `analyze_and_trade()` method needs to orchestrate these heartbeat calls - it should start one at the beginning of the cycle, then finish it at the end with the aggregated stats, and importantly it should handle the case where the start fails gracefully by still calling complete with a None heartbeat.\n\nWriting test cases... I see the test mocks both heartbeat methods, so even though the scheduler uses a real session, those calls won't actually hit the database—the mocks return None and do nothing respectively. The test then verifies that despite the mocked heartbeat operations, the asset processing still completes and increments the processed count to 1. I see the real issue now—the full cycle test uses the actual implementation, so both heartbeat methods need to work correctly and be called in the right order. I also need to fix the `DummySession.query()` mock to properly handle the `order_by` chain that gets called when checking for active positions. I'm realizing the upsert logic needs to query for existing heartbeat rows in this environment and update the most recent one rather than creating duplicates—the test shows that calling the method twice should result in only one row with the same ID both times.\n\nWriting the implementation... Writing database operations...\n\nThen I'm realizing there's a logic issue—the test expects `_upsert_heartbeat_complete` to be called even when the heartbeat is `None`, but my early return would prevent that from happening, so I need to reconsider how to handle the `None` case while still satisfying the test expectations. Writing the analyze_and_trade method...\n\nNow I'm realizing there's a tension between the test setup in `test_vje_scheduler_heartbeat_backend.py` which uses a real SQLite session versus `test_scheduler.py` which uses a `DummySession`, and I need to figure out how the existing tests handle this discrepancy. Still writing mock setup...\n\nActually, I realize `DummySession` is missing the `refresh` method entirely, which `_upsert_heartbeat_start` would need to call after creating a new heartbeat record.\n\nWait, let me trace through this more carefully. The `refresh` method does exist on `DummySession` and it assigns an id if one doesn't exist, so that part should work. The real issue is that when existing tests like `test_trading_scheduler_long_signal_executes_order` run, they'll now trigger `_upsert_heartbeat_start`, which means the heartbeat table gets populated during test execution.\n\nThen `_upsert_heartbeat_complete` gets called after the main loop, and it's trying to query the Trade table to get the last trade's id. The function updates the heartbeat's status, completion time, stats, and links it to the last trade if one exists.\n\nThe issue is that `self.db.query(Trade).order_by(Trade.id.desc()).first()` doesn't use `.filter()` first, so the mock chain isn't properly configured—`order_by()` returns an unconfigured Mock, and calling `first()` on that gives another Mock object instead of None. This means `last_trade` becomes a truthy Mock, and accessing `last_trade.id` would also return a Mock, which could cause unexpected behavior.\n\nI need to update the DummySession to handle `order_by()` being called directly on the query object, not just on the filtered result. But this is getting unwieldy because the mock setup was originally designed for a specific query pattern, and now I'm adding more variations. Let me look at what queries the scheduler actually uses to see if there's a better approach.\n\nI'm seeing that all the queries follow a similar pattern—they chain filter and order_by calls, then call first() to get a single result or nothing. The cleanest solution is to make the mock query object return itself for both filter and order_by, so the chain works regardless of the order, and have first() always return None since DummySession is meant to simulate no results.\n\nBuilding a self-returning mock...\n\nNow I'm checking which tests might be affected by this change. The validation tests don't touch the database at all, and the start/stop tests use DummySession but never actually call analyze_and_trade, so they shouldn't be impacted. But the transient error tests do call run_once which triggers analyze_and_trade, so I need to understand why those are currently passing if they're hitting the same database queries. Let me re-read the issue. The failing tests are:\n- `test_trading_scheduler_long_signal_executes_order`\n- `test_trading_scheduler_short_signal_executes_order`\n- `test_trading_scheduler_hold_signal_skips_execution`\n\nBut tests like `test_scheduler_transient_error_continue_with_next_asset` also call `run_once()`. Why is that one passing but the three above aren't?\n\nLet me look at `test_scheduler_transient_error_continue_with_next_asset` more carefully. For BTC, `get_ohlc` raises `TimeoutError`, which gets wrapped as a `DataFetchError` in the `_fetch_market_data` method. The error handler in `analyze_and_trade` catches this and increments the error counter, allowing the loop to continue to SPX. For SPX, `get_ohlc` returns valid data, and then `_process_asset` tries to fetch the active position from the session, which returns a Mock object since we're using DummySession. I'm wondering if those other tests are also failing but just weren't listed in the issue, or if there's something different about how they're set up that avoids triggering the same problem. Let me look more carefully at what the issue actually specifies versus what I'm inferring. Both tests use a single BTC asset and call `run_once()`, so why does one pass while the other fails? There must be something different in how they're setting up the mocks or what they're asserting that's causing the `Mock` object to leak into the position tracking logic. I see the issue now—the mock setup only handles the first `filter()` call, but `get_active_position()` chains additional methods like `order_by()` that aren't configured, so they return unconfigured Mock objects instead of the expected None or empty list. The test needs to mock the entire chain of method calls to properly simulate what the database query would return. are fixed and the currently-passing tests stay that way. The fix is straightforward: I need to make `DummySession.query()` return a self-referential mock so that chained calls like `query(...).filter(...).order_by(...).first()` work correctly and return None as expected. When I add the heartbeat methods to `analyze_and_trade`, they'll also benefit from this fix since they'll use the same DummySession. Now I'm checking whether `test_scheduler_transient_error_continue_with_next_asset` currently passes—the BTC asset raises a `TimeoutError` that gets caught before reaching `_process_asset`.\n\nActually, tracing through the flow more carefully: the error happens inside `_fetch_market_data` which is called from `_process_asset`, so the exception propagates up and gets caught in `analyze_and_trade` where it increments the error counter and moves to the next asset. This means `position_monitor.get_active_position` never gets called for BTC. For SPX, the data fetch succeeds and the flow continues to check the position.\n\nBut wait—if SPX hits that TypeError when updating candle tracking, that exception would also be caught by the outer exception handler, which would count it as an error rather than a successful process. Yet the test expects `processed == 1` and `errors == 1`, which suggests one asset succeeded and one failed. Let me look at the test assertion again to see if I'm misunderstanding what should happen. The test command uses marker filtering to skip integration and slow tests, so it stops after hitting 10 failures across both test files rather than running the full suite. The fix is straightforward—I need to add an `_upsert_heartbeat_start` method to the scheduler that queries for existing heartbeat records and creates or updates them as needed.\n\nI'll also add an `_upsert_heartbeat_complete` method to finalize the heartbeat with completion status and trade statistics, then integrate both into the `analyze_and_trade` method by calling the start method at the beginning and the complete method at the end. For the test file, I need to fix the test setup...\n\nFixing the mock query chain...\n\nI should add `SchedulerHeartbeat` to the top-level imports since it's in the same models module as `Signal` and `TradeSignal`, though I'll need to be careful about `Trade` since it's imported inline elsewhere to avoid circular dependencies. Let me check what the `_upsert_heartbeat_complete` method needs to do with the Trade query. The query would fetch the most recent trade globally by ordering descending on the id and taking the first result, which should work fine for this test since there's only one trade. Now I need to shift into the planning phase and create the implementation documents and artifacts.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1463,"cache_read_input_tokens":53996,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1463},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2b9b65c5-e77e-400b-90aa-d1fae3699661","uui